✅ 6. OOPs Concepts

Encapsulation: Wrapping data and methods into a single unit (class) and restricting direct access using access modifiers.
Inheritance: One class (child) inherits the properties and methods of another (parent), allowing code reuse.

Single → One parent, one child.
Multilevel → Child becomes parent for another class.
Hierarchical → Multiple children from one parent.
Multiple → Multiple parents (not directly supported in C#, but possible via interfaces).
Hybrid → Combination of the above.


Polymorphism: Same method behaves differently (overload/override).
🔹 Method Overloading or CompileTime Polymorphism :Same method
   name with different parameter types or counts.
🔹 Method Overriding or Run time Polymorphism : Child class provides a 
   new implementation of a virtual method from the parent class.


Abstraction: Hiding complex logic and showing only essentials using abstract classes or interfaces.
Use interfaces to achieve full abstraction — like defining transaction behavior across services.
Use abstract classes when you want to include shared logic along with required methods that subclasses must implement.

Real-Time Example: Payment System
Scenario:
You’re building a Payment Gateway module in an e-commerce system. All payment methods (Credit Card, UPI, PayPal, etc.) must:
Connect to the payment gateway (same for all)
Process the payment (different for each type)
Generate a receipt (same logic for all)

Common behavior (ConnectToGateway, GenerateReceipt) stays in the base class.
Specific behavior (ProcessPayment) is left for subclasses to implement.
You can add new payment types without changing existing code (Open/Closed Principle).
